+# Daily Grind phone verification
+
+Run `pnpm start` to serve the static site. The complete interaction is a demo: it does not send SMS or establish an authenticated account. Enter a valid national phone number, then use `123456`. Codes expire after five minutes, allow five verification attempts, and can be resent after 30 seconds. These limits are illustrative and client-side only.
+
+For production, replace `verificationService.send` and `verificationService.verify` in `app.js` with your backend endpoints. The server must normalize/validate the number, generate and hash random codes, enforce expiry, attempt and resend limits, consume codes once, and bind each challenge to the requesting session and number. Return an opaque challenge ID from send and a server-verified result from verify; establish authentication on the server if needed. Do not return the OTP to the client. Remove the demo-only code and copy when connecting real delivery.
+
+Serve over HTTPS. The outgoing message should be:
+
+```text
+Your Daily Grind verification code is 012345.
+
+@your-domain.example #012345
+```
+
+Use the actual requesting host, with no scheme, port, or path, on the final line. WebOTP is feature-detected and aborts on submit, resend, change number, timeout, and page exit. Unsupported browsers retain `autocomplete="one-time-code"` and manual/pasted entry into a single labeled text input.
